INT. SS HESPERIA, MAIL SORTING ROOM - NIGHT (1912)

A low steel room lined with pigeonholes, lit by green-shaded lamps. The ship rolls gently. Letters slide across tables.

PURSER EZEKIEL TRAVERS, 50s, sleeve garters, sorts a bundle by hand. Across from him, MINNIE ABARA, 20s, a clerk with ink on her cuff, stamps envelopes in a steady rhythm.

At a third table, FOSTER, 30s, in a brown wool suit, files telegrams. And at the back, QUINCEY, a boy of 12, sweeps the floor with a broom taller than he is.

TRAVERS
(to the room)
Gentlemen, ladies, we dock Thursday. I'd like the mail from Queenstown done by then.

FOSTER
It will be, sir.

MINNIE
(to herself)
Dublin, Dublin, Cork. Dublin.

TRAVERS
Miss Abara, how many left?

MINNIE
Four hundred and twelve, sir.

TRAVERS
That's a fair number.

MINNIE
It's a fair ship, sir.

Travers smiles at that. Returns to his sorting.

QUINCEY
(muttering)
Four hundred and eleven.

MINNIE
Quincey, what are you counting?

QUINCEY
You dropped one, miss.

He holds up a postcard. Minnie takes it, reads, and tucks it carefully in a pigeonhole.

FOSTER
(to Quincey)
Good eyes, lad.

QUINCEY
Thank you, sir.

FOSTER
(to himself)
Better than mine.

TRAVERS
(to everyone)
Tea, anyone?

MINNIE
Yes, please, sir.

FOSTER
No, thank you.

QUINCEY
Is there milk?

TRAVERS
There's always milk, Quincey. It's the one thing the sea doesn't take.

He sets down the bundle. Rises. Crosses to the kettle. The ship rolls. The lamps swing. Nobody notices.
